Magnesium-based Hydrides  Magnesium alloys have received attention as a potential hydrogen storage material due to their low density, high abundance, and relatively low cost [ 103 ]. Magnesium alloys have a hexa-crystalline structure that makes it possible to store 7.7 wt% hydrogen, which is much higher than compressed hydrogen storage (23 kgH.m −3  at pressures of 350 bar and 38 kgH.m −3  at 700 bar), and much higher than liquid hydrogen storage (71 kgH. m −3 ) [ 104 ]. The reaction is represented as: 1 Mg + H 2 → MgH 2 Mg + H 2 → MgH 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
